In the basic vignette, there's a section about using sass for dynamically inserting css into an app.

```
library(shiny)

ui <- fluidPage(
  headerPanel("Sass Color Example"),
  colourpicker::colourInput("color", "Background Color", value = "#6498d2", showColour = "text"),
  uiOutput("sass")
)

server <- function(input, output) {
  output$sass <- renderUI({
    tags$head(tags$style(css()))
  })
  css <- reactive({
    sass::sass(list(
      list(color = input$color),
      "body { background-color: $color; }"
    ))
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

It's not clear if there's any value added by adding the sass package here and making extra funtion calls over just using plain css

```
     css <- reactive({
         paste("body { background-color:", input$color, "}")
     })
```

Is there any advantage with sass for this example? If not, maybe it should be noted that plain css is just fine and sass should only be used when needed?

Currently a cache hit doesn't occur for the same Sass code if a different HTML dependency happens to be attached to it 

```r
library(sass)
library(htmltools)

my_layer <- function() {
  src <- tempfile()
  dir.create(src)
  sass_layer(
    "@function fib($x) {
       @if $x <= 1 {
         @return $x
       }
       @return fib($x - 2) + fib($x - 1);
     }
    
     body {
       width: fib(27);
     }",
    html_deps = htmlDependency("foo", "1.0", src)
  )
}

system.time(
  sass(my_layer())
)
#> user  system elapsed 
#> 1.720   0.005   1.729 
system.time(
  sass(my_layer())
)
#> user  system elapsed 
#> 1.716   0.004   1.723 
```

It currently (sort of) works with a warning because `file.exists()` is not vectorized, but everything else is:

```r
sass::sass_file <- function (input) {
    if (!file.exists(input)) {
        stop("Could not find file: '", input, "' in dir: ", getwd())
    }
    input <- normalizePath(input, mustWork = TRUE)
    ret <- sass_import(input)
    class(ret) <- c("sass_file", class(ret))
    attr(ret, "sass_file_path") <- input
    ret
}
```

Turns out `normalizePath()` can be slow, so we should try to speed this with vectorization
